            <ArticleTitle>A time-varying group sparse additive model for genome-wide association studies of dynamic complex traits.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2903-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/bioinformatics/btw347</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="MOTIVATION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Despite the widespread popularity of genome-wide association studies (GWAS) for genetic mapping of complex traits, most existing GWAS methodologies are still limited to the use of static phenotypes measured at a single time point. In this work, we propose a new method for association mapping that considers dynamic phenotypes measured at a sequence of time points. Our approach relies on the use of Time-Varying Group Sparse Additive Models (TV-GroupSpAM) for high-dimensional, functional regression.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">This new model detects a sparse set of genomic loci that are associated with trait dynamics, and demonstrates increased statistical power over existing methods. We evaluate our method via experiments on synthetic data and perform a proof-of-concept analysis for detecting single nucleotide polymorphisms associated with two phenotypes used to assess asthma severity: forced vital capacity, a sensitive measure of airway obstruction and bronchodilator response, which measures lung response to bronchodilator drugs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AVAILABILITY AND IMPLEMENTATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Source code for TV-GroupSpAM freely available for download at http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~mmarchet/projects/tv_group_spam, implemented in MATLAB.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTACT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">epxing@cs.cmu.edu</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUPPLEMENTARY INFORMATION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Supplementary data are available at Bioinformatics online.</AbstractText>
